    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDescribe how you measured the baseline, identified the bottleneck, changed one or more factors, and verified the result.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis is intended to reveal how you reason about an engineering situation, not just whether you know the vocabulary. A strong answer makes the assumption, decision, and outcome explicit.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Define the situation or goal.\n2. Explain the decision or mechanism.\n3. Give the evidence or example that supports it.\n4. State the limitation, trade-off, or follow-up action.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer focused on the actual situation, then explain the decision and why I made it. I would finish with the result or lesson and let the interviewer decide whether to go deeper.\n\n**Example :**\nFor an API slowdown, compare trace timings, query duration, CPU, and cache hit rate before and after the change rather than claiming it “felt faster.”\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nPerformance answers are strongest when the improvement is tied to a measurable metric and a known bottleneck.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving a hypothetical story when a real example is expected.\n- Focusing on the team outcome without explaining your own contribution.\n- Leaving out the reasoning behind the decision.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the key idea first, then justify it with the invariant, constraint, or decision rule that makes the answer correct.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDescribe how you measured the baseline, identified the bottleneck, changed one or more factors, and verified the result.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis is intended to reveal how you reason about an engineering situation, not just whether you know the vocabulary. A strong answer makes the assumption, decision, and outcome explicit.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Define the situation or goal.\n2. Explain the decision or mechanism.\n3. Give the evidence or example that supports it.\n4. State the limitation, trade-off, or follow-up action.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer focused on the actual situation, then explain the decision and why I made it. I would finish with the result or lesson and let the interviewer decide whether to go deeper.\n\n**Example :**\nFor an API slowdown, compare trace timings, query duration, CPU, and cache hit rate before and after the change rather than claiming it “felt faster.”\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nPerformance answers are strongest when the improvement is tied to a measurable metric and a known bottleneck.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving a hypothetical story when a real example is expected.\n- Focusing on the team outcome without explaining your own contribution.\n- Leaving out the reasoning behind the decision.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the key idea first, then justify it with the invariant, constraint, or decision rule that makes the answer correct.",
