    slug: "netflix-design-ride-matching-87",
    shortDescription: "Design a real-time driver-rider matching system.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRide matching assigns available drivers to requests using location, eligibility, pricing, and fairness constraints. Real-time state and concurrency are central because driver availability changes quickly.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks architecture judgment: requirements, bottlenecks, failure modes, consistency, and operational cost. The best answer explains why the components fit together instead of naming many services.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Clarify the core functional requirement.\n2. State the most important scale and reliability assumptions.\n3. Describe the request or data flow.\n4. Identify the bottleneck, failure mode, and key trade-off.\n5. Explain how you would validate the design.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would start with the requirement and the assumption that drives the architecture. Then I would walk through the main request or data flow, call out the likely bottleneck and failure mode, and finish with how I would measure the design in production.\n\n**Example :**\nA request in a geographic cell queries nearby available drivers, reserves one atomically, and confirms the match before sending the trip to the driver.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nThe design must handle stale location data, duplicate requests, concurrent claims, and sudden demand spikes.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Drawing components before stating requirements.\n- Claiming that a system “scales” without identifying what limits it.\n- Ignoring consistency, retries, idempotency, observability, or operational ownership.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the key idea first, then justify it with the invariant, constraint, or decision rule that makes the answer correct.",
