    shortDescription: "Explain immutable objects.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAn immutable object cannot change its observable state after construction. Updates create a new value rather than mutating the existing instance.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you understand the design or language idea well enough to use it, not just repeat its definition. The interviewer can probe coupling, maintainability, runtime behavior, or language constraints.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Define the concept in one sentence.\n2. Explain the language or design rule behind it.\n3. Show the consequence with a small example.\n4. State the main trade-off or limitation.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define the concept first, then explain why the language or design uses that model. I would give one concrete example and finish with the limitation I would keep in mind in production.\n\n**Example :**\nA Money value object can expose amount and currency as read-only state and return a new Money when adding another amount.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nImmutability reduces aliasing bugs and makes concurrent or cached values easier to reason about, at the cost of additional object creation in some designs.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing a language feature with a design principle.\n- Giving a textbook definition without explaining the consequence in code.\n- Presenting a guideline as an absolute rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the key idea first, then justify it with the invariant, constraint, or decision rule that makes the answer correct.",
